        "id": "23",
        "title": "Common Mistakes to Avoid When Using Ai Business Card Maker",
        "slug": "common-mistakes-to-avoid-when-using-ai-business-card-maker",
        "excerpt": "Learn the frequent design, content, and output errors to avoid when using an AI to generate your business cards, ensuring print-readiness and professionalism.",
        "content": "<h2>Pitfalls in Automated Card Design</h2>\n\n<p>An AI Business Card Maker is a powerful automation tool, but it relies on accurate human input. Misuse can lead to cards that are visually unprofessional or fail to print correctly. Avoid these common mistakes for a perfect outcome.</p>\n\n<h3>Mistake 1: Ignoring Bleed and Safety Margins</h3>\n\n<h4>The Error</h4>\n\n<p>Treating the card preview as the final cut. During printing, slight shifts require 'bleed' (extra design area) and 'safety margins' (where content must reside). Ignoring these leads to text being cut off or borders being uneven.</p>\n\n<h4>The Fix</h4>\n\n<p>Always use the AI tool's export options that include bleed guides. Keep critical information (names, numbers) within the defined safety margin.</p>\n\n<h3>Mistake 2: Low-Resolution Logo Upload</h3>\n\n<h4>The Error</h4>\n\n<p>Uploading a web-optimized, low-resolution PNG or JPG logo. When printed, the logo will look blurry or pixelated, undermining professionalism.</p>\n\n<h4>The Fix</h4>\n\n<p><strong>Always upload a vector file (SVG, EPS, or AI) or a high-resolution PNG (300 DPI or higher)</strong> to ensure crispness on the final printed card.</p>\n\n<h3>Mistake 3: Poor Color Contrast for Printing</h3>\n\n<h4>The Error</h4>\n\n<p>Selecting colors that look good on screen (RGB) but print poorly (CMYK). Light text on a slightly darker background may fail to register clearly on a matte print.</p>\n\n<h4>The Fix</h4>\n\n<p>Verify the final color export settings are <strong>CMYK</strong> (Cyan, Magenta, Yellow, Key/Black) and use the AI tool's <strong>contrast checker</strong> to ensure readability. [Image of CMYK color separation diagram]</p>\n\n<h3>Mistake 4: Missing Crucial Information</h3>\n\n<h4>The Error</h4>\n\n<p>Focusing too much on design and forgetting key contact details (e.g., a relevant social media handle or the company's full legal name).</p>\n\n<h4>The Fix</h4>\n\n<p>Use a checklist during the input phase: Full Name, Job Title, Phone, Email, Website, and relevant Company Tagline/Address.</p>",
        "author": "Doodax Team",
        "date": "2025-11-27T04:45:00Z",
        "category": "Insights",
        "tags": [],
        "faq": [
            {
                "question": "What is 'bleed' in printing terms?",
                "answer": "Bleed is the extra space around the edge of a document that ensures the design extends past the cut line. This prevents white edges from showing up on the final card if the cutter shifts slightly during the printing process."
            },
            {
                "question": "Why can't I use an RGB color file for printing?",
                "answer": "RGB (Red, Green, Blue) is used for screens and monitors. Professional printing uses CMYK (Cyan, Magenta, Yellow, Black) inks. Sending an RGB file to a printer often results in dull or inaccurate color conversion."
            }
        ],
