        "excerpt": "The definitive guide to interactive color tools, covering color theory (monochromatic, analogous), format translation, and advanced use in theme generation.",
        "content": "<h2>Decoding the Language of Hue, Saturation, and Lightness</h2>\n\n<p>Interactive color tools provide a centralized hub for all color-related tasks in design and development. This guide covers the basics of color harmony for beginners and the advanced technical compliance required by expert design system architects.</p>\n\n<h3>I. Beginner: Color Harmony and Theory</h3>\n\n<h4>A. The Color Wheel</h4>\n\n<p>Beginners should use the tool to learn basic color theory: <strong>Complementary</strong> (colors opposite each other, creating high visual contrast) and <strong>Analogous</strong> (colors next to each other, creating visual harmony).</p>\n\n<h4>B. Mood and Emotion</h4>\n\n<p>Use the tool to match colors to the desired mood (e.g., Blues/Greens for trust and stability, Reds/Oranges for energy and urgency). This ensures the interface color supports the business goal.</p>\n\n<h3>II. Expert: Technical Compliance and Formats</h3>\n\n<h4>A. Color Naming Conventions</h4>\n\n<p><strong>Expert Tip:</strong> Use the tool to generate consistent color variable names (e.g., `primary-600`, `secondary-200`). This is crucial for managing large design systems and ensuring developers use the correct shade/tint in code.</p>\n\n<h4>B. Gamut Check (Print vs. Screen)</h4>\n\n<p>Advanced tools can check if a high-saturation color selected for the screen ($\text{RGB}$) is accurately renderable in the smaller color space of $\text{CMYK}$ (print). This prevents unexpected dullness in printed marketing assets.</p>\n\n<h3>III. Advanced Use Cases</h3>\n\n<ul><li><strong>Dark Mode Theme Generation:</strong> Generate the secondary palette required for dark mode (typically inverting the L-value of the $\text{HSL}$ model) while maintaining the original hue and saturation values.</li><li><strong>Component State Colors:</strong> Defining the precise color for interactive states: `hover`, `active`, and `disabled`, ensuring they are visually distinct but harmonized with the main palette.</li></ul>\n<strong>Integration:</strong> Exporting the final palette as a $\text{JSON}$ or $\text{SASS}$ map provides a single source of truth for all color variables across the project.",
        "author": "Doodax Team",
        "date": "2025-11-27T13:32:00Z",
