        "excerpt": "A deep dive into Will Generator features, including executor designation, specific and residuary bequest definition, and multi-state compliance checks.",
        "content": "<h2>The Framework for Final Wishes</h2>\n\n<p>A $\text{Powered}$ $\text{Will}$ $\text{Generator}$ is a guided legal document automation tool. Its features are designed to walk the user through complex legal terminology and ensure the document is complete, legally sound, and addresses all necessary aspects of estate planning.</p>\n\n<h3>I. Key Features</h3>\n\n<h4>A. Executor and Guardian Designation</h4>\n\n<p><strong>Feature:</strong> The tool provides clear sections to name both a <strong>Primary Executor</strong> (the person managing the estate) and a <strong>Contingent Guardian</strong> for any minor children.</p>\n\n<h4>B. Bequest Definition</h4>\n\n<p><strong>Benefit:</strong> Allows the user to define two types of gifts:\n<ul><li><strong>Specific Bequests:</strong> Clearly named gifts of personal property to specific individuals.</li><li><strong>Residuary Bequests:</strong> Defines how the remainder of the estate (after debts and specific gifts are paid) should be divided, often by percentage.</li></ul>\n<h4>C. State Compliance</h4></p>\n\n<p><strong>Crucial Feature:</strong> The generator should update its legal language and execution requirements (witnesses, notarization) based on the user's state of residence, minimizing the risk of a void document.</p>\n\n<h3>II. Use Cases</h3>\n\n<ul><li><strong>Young Families:</strong> Essential for newly married couples or parents of minors who need to designate guardianship quickly.</li><li><strong>Simple Estates:</strong> Ideal for individuals with straightforward assets ($\text{bank}$ $\text{accounts}$, $\text{a}$ $\text{single}$ $\text{home}$) who need basic protection.</li><li><strong>Contingency Planning:</strong> Used to formally document wishes for funeral arrangements and final disposition of remains.</li></ul>\n<h3>III. Security and Final Execution</h3>\n\n<p><strong>Best Practice:</strong> The generator should remind the user that the Will is *not* legally valid until it is physically printed and signed by the testator and the required witnesses (and notarized, if applicable) according to state law.</p>",
        "author": "Doodax Team",
        "date": "2025-11-27T14:19:00Z",
        "category": "Insights",
