        "id": "201",
        "title": "Common Mistakes to Avoid When Using a Random Number Generator",
        "slug": "common-mistakes-to-avoid-when-using-a-random-number-generator",
        "excerpt": "Identify major errors in using random number generators: confusing security vs. statistical randomness, using low-entropy seeds, and poor range definition.",
        "content": "<h2>Security vs. Statistical Randomness</h2>\n\n<p>$\text{Random}$ $\text{Number}$ $\text{Generators}$ ($\text{RNGs}$) are fundamental to computing, used for everything from statistical modeling to cryptographic security. The biggest mistake is confusing <strong>statistically random</strong> ($\text{sufficient}$ $\text{for}$ $\text{games}$) with <strong>cryptographically secure random</strong> ($\text{mandatory}$ $\text{for}$ $\text{security}$).</p>\n\n<h3>Mistake 1: Confusing $\text{PRNG}$ and $\text{CSPRNG}$</h3>\n\n<h4>The Error</h4>\n\n<p>Using a $\text{Pseudo}$-$\text{Random}$ $\text{Number}$ $\text{Generator}$ ($\text{PRNG}$) for security-critical tasks (e.g., generating user $\text{passwords}$ $\text{or}$ $\text{encryption}$ $\text{keys}$). $\text{PRNGs}$ $\text{are}$ $\text{predictable}$.</p>\n\n<h4>The Fix</h4>\n\n<p><strong>Mandatory:</strong> For security tasks, use a $\text{Cryptographically}$ $\text{Secure}$ $\text{PRNG}$ ($\text{CSPRNG}$). $\text{CSPRNGs}$ $\text{rely}$ $\text{on}$ $\text{high}$-$\text{entropy}$ $\text{system}$ $\text{sources}$ ($\text{hardware}$ $\text{noise}$) to ensure the output is practically unpredictable.</p>\n\n<h3>Mistake 2: Poor Range Definition</h3>\n\n<h4>The Error</h4>\n\n<p>Generating a random number where the maximum and minimum values are not correctly defined, leading to unexpected outliers or numbers outside the required range.</p>\n\n<h4>The Fix</h4>\n\n<p>Clearly define the inclusive ($\text{min}$ $\text{and}$ $\text{max}$ $\text{are}$ $\text{included}$) or exclusive ($\text{max}$ $\text{is}$ $\text{excluded}$) boundaries of the number generation.</p>\n\n<h3>Mistake 3: Using Low-Entropy Seeds</h3>\n\n<h4>The Error</h4>\n\n<p>Manually defining the seed value (the starting point for the sequence) as a simple number (e.g., $1$). This makes the entire sequence predictable.</p>\n\n<h4>The Fix</h4>\n\n<p><strong>Best Practice:</strong> Use the system's current $\text{time}$ ($\text{milliseconds}$), $\text{hardware}$ $\text{noise}$, or a true hardware $\text{RNG}$ source as the seed for maximum unpredictability.</p>\n\n<h3>Mistake 4: Generating Non-Uniform Distribution</h3>\n\n<h4>The Error</h4>\n\n<p>Using a generator that favors certain numbers in the range, skewing the statistical analysis.</p>\n\n<h4>The Fix</h4>\n\n<p>Verify the tool uses an algorithm (like the $\text{Mersenne}$ $\text{Twister}$ for $\text{PRNG}$) that ensures a uniform distribution across the entire range.</p>",
        "author": "Doodax Team",
        "date": "2025-11-27T14:34:00Z",
        "category": "Insights",
        "tags": [],
        "faq": [
            {
                "question": "What is the difference between random and pseudorandom?",
                "answer": "**Random** means the number is truly unpredictable (usually generated by hardware noise). **Pseudorandom** means the number is generated by an algorithm and is predictable if you know the starting value ($\text{seed}$)."
            },
            {
                "question": "When should I use a simple pseudorandom generator?",
                "answer": "Simple $\text{PRNGs}$ are fine for non-security tasks like shuffling a playlist, generating random colors for a chart, or creating basic visual effects in a video game."
            }
        ],
        "relatedTools": []
